ch:\n\n1. **Mercury**  \n   Despite being the closest planet to the Sun, Mercury is not the hottest planet. Its lack of a significant atmosphere means it cannot retain heat, causing temperatures to plummet to -180°C (-290°F) at night.\n\n2. **Venus**  \n   Venus rotates in the opposite direction to most other planets (retrograde rotation), meaning the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east. It also has the slowest rotation of any planet, taking 243 Earth days to complete one spin.\n\n3. **Earth**  \n   Earth is the only planet known to support life. It is also the densest planet in the solar system and the only one not named after a Greek or Roman god.\n\n4. **Mars**  \n   Mars is home to Olympus Mons, the tallest volcano in the solar system. It stands about 21.9 km (13.6 miles) high, nearly three times the height of Mount Everest.\n\n5. **Jupiter**  \n   Jupiter has the shortest day of any planet in the solar system", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.39222410000365926, "decode_s": 2.1247184999956517, "total_s": 2.5171201000048313, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 120.01589857692765, "pp": 66.28863448155641}}
